WEBVTT

00:30.000 --> 00:54.000
🎵

00:54.000 --> 00:59.500
🎵Can you stop a turkey boy, Billy boy, Billy boy?🎵

00:59.500 --> 01:04.500
🎵Can you stop a turkey good for Thanksgiving?🎵

01:04.500 --> 01:09.500
🎵She can stop a turkey good like a turkey stopper should🎵

01:09.500 --> 01:15.500
🎵But she's a young thing and cannot leave her mother🎵

01:15.500 --> 01:19.500
🎵Can she roast a turkey brown?🎵

01:19.500 --> 01:26.500
🎵

01:26.500 --> 01:29.500
Don't pay no attention to what Granny's singing, Turkey.

01:29.500 --> 01:31.500
Where'd you go?

01:31.500 --> 01:33.500
Here, Turkey, Turkey.

01:33.500 --> 01:36.500
Here, Turkey, Turkey. Don't be scared.

01:36.500 --> 01:38.500
Granny don't mean no harm.

01:38.500 --> 01:40.500
See, I brought you some nice stuffing.

01:40.500 --> 01:42.500
I mean bread.

01:42.500 --> 01:45.500
🎵Is she fixing gravy too?🎵

01:45.500 --> 01:50.500
🎵Billy boy, Billy boy, fixing giblet gravy too for Thanksgiving🎵

01:50.500 --> 01:54.500
🎵Yes, she's fixing gravy too like her mama used to do🎵

01:54.500 --> 01:59.500
🎵She's a young thing and cannot leave her mother🎵

01:59.500 --> 02:02.500
🎵Can she make a pumpkin pie, Billy?🎵

02:04.500 --> 02:05.500
Hold on, Ellie.

02:05.500 --> 02:07.500
Oh, morning, Tom.

02:07.500 --> 02:09.500
What you got there?

02:09.500 --> 02:12.500
Oh, this here's a raccoon named Elmer.

02:12.500 --> 02:13.500
Give it a name.

02:13.500 --> 02:14.500
Yes, sir, Tom.

02:14.500 --> 02:18.500
I know that's a raccoon named Elmer. What you got in the sack?

02:18.500 --> 02:19.500
What sack?

02:19.500 --> 02:20.500
Sack of token.

02:20.500 --> 02:22.500
Sack of token?

02:22.500 --> 02:25.500
Yeah, the sack that Turkey just stuck his head out of.

02:27.500 --> 02:29.500
Don't you stay out of sight.

02:29.500 --> 02:30.500
Hey, come here.

02:30.500 --> 02:32.500
Yes, sir, Pop.

02:37.500 --> 02:42.500
Ellie, may I told you not to go friendly enough with that turkey bird so close before Thanksgiving?

02:42.500 --> 02:44.500
Well, it wasn't me, Pop. It was Elmer here.

02:44.500 --> 02:47.500
Was it Elmer who chucked him in that sack?

02:47.500 --> 02:50.500
Well, he didn't do it alone. I kind of helped him.

02:50.500 --> 02:53.500
I kind of helped him take him out and put him back in his pen.

02:53.500 --> 02:56.500
Mr. Drysdale gave us that bird for a meal, not a pen.

02:56.500 --> 02:59.500
He's awful smart, Pop, and friendly, too.

02:59.500 --> 03:01.500
Well, I learned him to shake hands.

03:01.500 --> 03:05.500
Well, Ellie, he ain't likely to be going into politics.

03:05.500 --> 03:08.500
I'll just take him out of the sack and he can shake hands with you.

03:08.500 --> 03:10.500
I don't want to shake his hand.

03:10.500 --> 03:13.500
What's going to happen to him is bad enough without him thinking a friend done it to him.

03:13.500 --> 03:15.500
Put him back and leave him be.

03:15.500 --> 03:20.500
Sir, Pop, come on, Elmer, let's put Paul Herman back in the pen.

03:21.500 --> 03:23.500
Herman.

03:23.500 --> 03:28.500
I declare, Duke, if Mr. Drysdale give us a string of weenies, that girl will make pets out of him.

03:30.500 --> 03:34.500
Jed, can I borrow that old hound dog for a minute?

03:34.500 --> 03:35.500
Sure, Granny. What for?

03:35.500 --> 03:39.500
The turkey got away, and I want old Duke to sniff out his trail.

03:39.500 --> 03:41.500
Oh, you don't need old Duke, Granny.

03:41.500 --> 03:43.500
Yes, I do, Jed.

03:43.500 --> 03:46.500
My nose ain't what it used to be.

03:46.500 --> 03:50.500
It was Ellie who let Herman out of the turkey.

03:50.500 --> 03:51.500
Herman?

03:51.500 --> 03:55.500
Don't tell me she's making a pet out of our Thanksgiving riddles, is she?

03:55.500 --> 03:57.500
No, no, she's putting him back.

03:57.500 --> 03:59.500
Well, that's better.

03:59.500 --> 04:04.500
It's been a whole year since the guys they all sat down at the table with us, and we got to put on the dog.

04:04.500 --> 04:06.500
Oh.

04:07.500 --> 04:09.500
Don't worry, Duke.

04:09.500 --> 04:12.500
When folks says they're putting on a dog, it just means they're doing things fancy.

04:12.500 --> 04:14.500
Yeah, Duke.

04:14.500 --> 04:19.500
Like dressing up in your Sunday go-to-meeting vest, and having middles in the fancy eating room.

04:19.500 --> 04:24.500
That reminds me, I told Jester I would have put the company chairs around the fancy eating table.

04:24.500 --> 04:26.500
Let's go in and see how he's doing.

04:26.500 --> 04:29.500
Didn't aim to scare you, Duke.

04:29.500 --> 04:34.500
Come on, now. Get them worry-winkles out of your head.

04:34.500 --> 04:41.500
I told folks it's got to stick together.

04:50.500 --> 04:57.500
Uncle Jed, Granny, Ellie, me, Mr. Drysdale, Mrs. Drysdale, Miss Jane.

04:57.500 --> 04:59.500
How's it coming, Jethro?

04:59.500 --> 05:01.500
Oh, fine, Uncle Jed.

05:01.500 --> 05:05.500
All right, Doggy, there's one thing you got to say about the folks out here.

05:05.500 --> 05:08.500
They believe in building a good, strong eating table.

05:08.500 --> 05:13.500
Why, you could serve up a whole barbecued steer on this thing and not blow it down.

05:13.500 --> 05:14.500
Yes, sir.

05:14.500 --> 05:17.500
You remember that little fella that came home from school with me the other day?

05:17.500 --> 05:18.500
Yeah.

05:18.500 --> 05:21.500
Well, he says this here room is what you call a billiard room.

05:21.500 --> 05:25.500
And this here table is what you call a billiard table.

05:25.500 --> 05:26.500
Hmm.

05:26.500 --> 05:28.500
And you know what I got figured out?

05:28.500 --> 05:29.500
What?

05:29.500 --> 05:34.500
This here rascal must be what you call a billiard.

05:34.500 --> 05:39.500
Eh, Doggy, I always wondered what that critter was.

05:39.500 --> 05:41.500
Pain-looking, ain't he?

05:41.500 --> 05:42.500
That's a fact.

05:42.500 --> 05:46.500
Judging from the size of his head, he must be monstrous big.

05:46.500 --> 05:49.500
I reckon that's why they had to build an extra-strong table.

05:49.500 --> 05:52.500
You reckon we could shoot one of these sometime?

05:52.500 --> 05:55.500
If he'd live around here in Beverly Hills?

05:55.500 --> 05:56.500
I reckon so.

05:56.500 --> 06:01.500
This little fella says his pa shoots billiards a couple nights a week.

06:01.500 --> 06:03.500
And of the night, do they?

06:03.500 --> 06:04.500
Yes, sir.

06:04.500 --> 06:08.500
I ain't so sure I'd want to run into him in the dark.

06:08.500 --> 06:10.500
Awful ugly, huh?

06:10.500 --> 06:13.500
Well, maybe that's one good thing about catching us a billiard.

06:13.500 --> 06:19.500
I don't reckon even Elliot would want to make a pet out of him.

06:21.500 --> 06:24.500
What's that turkey doing in my sink?

06:24.500 --> 06:27.500
Well, he's just watching me stir him up a pan of grits, Granny.

06:27.500 --> 06:29.500
With my gravy?

06:29.500 --> 06:32.500
He's right partial to your grits and gravy, Granny.

06:32.500 --> 06:34.500
Why, he'd like to shake your hand.

06:34.500 --> 06:37.500
Well, I might be pleased to...

06:37.500 --> 06:39.500
Get that bird out of here.

06:39.500 --> 06:43.500
And I don't want to see him in my kitchen until he's dressed and ready for the oven.

06:43.500 --> 06:46.500
Oh, that kind of talk gets him all scared.

06:46.500 --> 06:48.500
Gravy was just a-greeting you, Herman.

06:48.500 --> 06:50.500
No, I wasn't.

06:50.500 --> 06:55.500
Now you take him out to the pen like your pa told you, and take his last meal with him.

06:57.500 --> 06:59.500
Shake hands with the turkey.

06:59.500 --> 07:02.500
I ain't grabbing no drumstick that grabs back at me.

07:02.500 --> 07:07.500
Look, Granny, Herman's a-strutting his feathers for you.

07:11.500 --> 07:13.500
Both of you, get out of here.

07:16.500 --> 07:18.500
Well, Granny, I'm ready for that gobbler.

07:18.500 --> 07:20.500
Praise be.

07:20.500 --> 07:25.500
If we leave him around Ellie much longer, he'll be wanting dessert and coffee with his meals.

07:26.500 --> 07:27.500
All right now, Herman.

07:27.500 --> 07:29.500
You ain't going to let him scare you no more.

07:29.500 --> 07:31.500
Is it a deal?

07:33.500 --> 07:34.500
Attaboy.

07:34.500 --> 07:37.500
Now you do your best to make friends with everybody.

07:40.500 --> 07:43.500
Oh, uh, Hallie Mae, would you do me a favor?

07:43.500 --> 07:44.500
Well, sure, Paul.

07:44.500 --> 07:50.500
Would you, uh, shinny up that walnut tree out back there and pick me a nice big sack of nuts?

07:50.500 --> 07:53.500
I sure will, Paul, and you can visit with Herman.

07:53.500 --> 07:54.500
Yeah.

07:54.500 --> 07:55.500
Hmm?

07:55.500 --> 07:57.500
Oh, uh.

07:58.500 --> 08:05.500
Well now, uh, Herman, uh, Mr. Gobbler, I reckon the quicker we get this over with, the better.

08:06.500 --> 08:07.500
Oh, I'm sorry.

08:07.500 --> 08:14.500
I don't usually refuse a hand offered in friendship, but in this case, I just got a feeling it wouldn't be lasting.

08:19.500 --> 08:20.500
No hard feelings.

08:20.500 --> 08:22.500
I got nothing against you.

08:22.500 --> 08:25.500
As turkeys go, I reckon you're right up there with the best.

08:28.500 --> 08:31.500
I'd appreciate it if you wouldn't look at me like that.

08:32.500 --> 08:33.500
Oh, what's the use?

08:33.500 --> 08:37.500
I can't do it.

08:39.500 --> 08:43.500
I reckon we just got to shoot us one of them billiards for Thanksgiving.

08:53.500 --> 08:56.500
That there is what you call a Beverly Hills billiard.

08:56.500 --> 08:58.500
I don't care what you call it.

08:58.500 --> 09:00.500
I ain't gonna cook it.

09:00.500 --> 09:04.500
Now, Granny, the folks that lived here before us must have found it pretty tasty.

09:04.500 --> 09:07.500
This here table was built just special for billiards.

09:07.500 --> 09:09.500
I don't care.

09:09.500 --> 09:11.500
Anything that looks that mean got a taste mean.

09:11.500 --> 09:14.500
It's too big to cook.

09:17.500 --> 09:19.500
You give Barbecue Pit our back.

09:19.500 --> 09:20.500
No, sir.

09:20.500 --> 09:24.500
Mr. Rice, they'll give us a turkey, and I got the stuffin' made.

09:24.500 --> 09:30.500
And by days, I want that turkey cleaned and dressed to no more foolishness.

09:31.500 --> 09:33.500
I'm sorry, Granny, I just can't do it.

09:33.500 --> 09:35.500
Maybe Jethro can.

09:35.500 --> 09:37.500
He ain't got acquainted with Herman like I have.

09:39.500 --> 09:40.500
Do what, Granny?

09:40.500 --> 09:41.500
Clean and dress a turkey.

09:41.500 --> 09:42.500
Can you do it?

09:42.500 --> 09:43.500
I reckon so.

09:43.500 --> 09:44.500
Well, get at it.

09:44.500 --> 09:46.500
It's back of the kitchen in that pen.

09:46.500 --> 09:47.500
Yes, ma'am.

09:47.500 --> 09:48.500
I declare.

09:48.500 --> 09:52.500
I never thought I'd see the day the Jet Clampett were scared to kill a turkey.

09:52.500 --> 09:56.500
Heaven help him if he ever meets up with a billiard.

10:00.500 --> 10:02.500
Hey, Granny, I got the turkey all cleaned and dressed.

10:02.500 --> 10:03.500
Get your man somewhere else.

10:03.500 --> 10:04.500
Yes, ma'am.

10:13.500 --> 10:15.500
Put him in the basement.

10:15.500 --> 10:18.500
What in tarnation?

10:18.500 --> 10:20.500
Do you call that dressing a turkey?

10:20.500 --> 10:22.500
Well, I did the best I could.

10:22.500 --> 10:24.500
I didn't have no clothes that would fit him.

10:24.500 --> 10:27.500
I borrowed that clothes from a little fella down the street.

10:29.500 --> 10:31.500
I think he's kinda cute myself.

10:31.500 --> 10:32.500
So does he.

10:32.500 --> 10:34.500
He even shook my hand.

10:38.500 --> 10:39.500
Hi there, Miss Jean.

10:39.500 --> 10:41.500
Greetings, Mr. Clampett.

10:41.500 --> 10:44.500
I wonder if I might borrow one of Granny's ancient outdoor cooking pots.

10:44.500 --> 10:46.500
Boy, you bet you can.

10:46.500 --> 10:48.500
But ain't you taking Thanksgiving bittles with us?

10:48.500 --> 10:49.500
Oh, yes, indeed.

10:49.500 --> 10:53.500
I merely wanted to borrow the venerable vessel as a photographic prop.

10:53.500 --> 10:57.500
You see, Mr. and Mrs. Drysdale are posing as Governor and Mrs. Bradford

10:57.500 --> 11:02.500
in a recreation of the first Thanksgiving some 342 years ago

11:02.500 --> 11:05.500
when the old gonkwins Quanto and Massasoit,

11:05.500 --> 11:06.500
a friend of the believers,

11:06.500 --> 11:11.500
pilgrims and so made it possible to perpetuate the Plymouth colony

11:11.500 --> 11:14.500
and bring them to be what is now one of the most important holidays

11:14.500 --> 11:16.500
in the American calendar.

11:20.500 --> 11:22.500
What was it you wanted again?

11:22.500 --> 11:23.500
The pots.

11:23.500 --> 11:26.500
Oh, yeah. I'll fetch it right up.

11:31.500 --> 11:33.500
You're very kind.

11:33.500 --> 11:36.500
Gilbert! Harry!

11:36.500 --> 11:38.500
Oh, here you are.

11:38.500 --> 11:40.500
So sorry for the delay.

11:40.500 --> 11:42.500
Miss Hathaway is out getting a kettle

11:42.500 --> 11:46.500
and Mr. Drysdale doesn't have his costume on as yet.

11:49.500 --> 11:53.500
Pale-faced Squaw, descendant of Pilgrim.

11:53.500 --> 11:57.500
He eat present when Red Brother bring turkey for feast.

11:57.500 --> 12:00.500
Red Brother bring turkey for feast.

12:03.500 --> 12:06.500
Me grateful to Red Brother.

12:09.500 --> 12:12.500
Newborn, hurry!

12:13.500 --> 12:14.500
What was she talking about?

12:14.500 --> 12:18.500
As near as I can figure she's got a brother who's a communist.

12:21.500 --> 12:23.500
What nation you from?

12:23.500 --> 12:25.500
You from Mohican?

12:25.500 --> 12:28.500
Kedek? Algonquin?

12:29.500 --> 12:31.500
Where you from?

12:31.500 --> 12:33.500
Central Casting.

12:38.500 --> 12:40.500
Here you are, Miss Jean.

12:40.500 --> 12:43.500
Now you got somebody to help you unload it over to the dry deal?

12:43.500 --> 12:45.500
Oh, yes, and thank you. I'm so grateful.

12:45.500 --> 12:46.500
My pleasure.

12:46.500 --> 12:48.500
See, maybe you can tell me something.

12:48.500 --> 12:49.500
Gladly.

12:49.500 --> 12:53.500
Whereabouts would a fellow go around here to shoot himself a billiard?

12:53.500 --> 12:57.500
Billiard? Oh, you mean the game, shoot billiards?

12:57.500 --> 12:58.500
Yes, ma'am.

12:58.500 --> 13:02.500
Ordinarily one would go to a billiard parlor.

13:02.500 --> 13:04.500
Parlor? Indoor?

13:04.500 --> 13:05.500
Definitely.

13:05.500 --> 13:07.500
Oh, not a parlor as you know it, Mr. Clampett.

13:07.500 --> 13:12.500
No, it's a large room with several tables and people come in and shoot billiards.

13:12.500 --> 13:13.500
Indoor?

13:13.500 --> 13:16.500
Yes. You see, the tables on which...

13:16.500 --> 13:19.500
Wait a minute. You have one in your billiard room.

13:19.500 --> 13:23.500
Yes, ma'am, and I got to see it's the ugliest thing I ever did see.

13:23.500 --> 13:26.500
Well, that's a matter of individual taste.

13:26.500 --> 13:29.500
They're quite expensive and many Beverly Hills mansions have one.

13:29.500 --> 13:34.500
Usually you'll find them in the playroom or the game room or the billiard room.

13:34.500 --> 13:36.500
But they keep them in the house.

13:36.500 --> 13:37.500
Always.

13:37.500 --> 13:40.500
Sure would have figured billiards for outdoors.

13:40.500 --> 13:42.500
Never out in the weather, no.

13:42.500 --> 13:44.500
Oh, dry deals are waiting.

13:44.500 --> 13:46.500
I'll see you later.

13:46.500 --> 13:49.500
Bye.

13:53.500 --> 13:55.500
Granny, would you believe it?

13:55.500 --> 14:01.500
These Beverly Hills folks keep them big, ugly billiards indoors and for pets.

14:01.500 --> 14:02.500
No.

14:02.500 --> 14:05.500
Yeah. Don't that take the rag off in the bush?

14:05.500 --> 14:06.500
Sure does.

14:06.500 --> 14:09.500
Now you go and take the head off of that gobbler.

14:09.500 --> 14:14.500
I just can't do that to a critter that keeps wanting to shake my hand.

14:14.500 --> 14:16.500
Here's the matter with you.

14:16.500 --> 14:20.500
Back home you used to bring home a great big gobbler every Thanksgiving.

14:20.500 --> 14:23.500
Yeah, but they was wild turkeys that are hunted and shot out in the woods.

14:23.500 --> 14:24.500
They had a sporting chance.

14:24.500 --> 14:29.500
Well, then you take iron out and turn it loose in the woods and hunt it.

14:29.500 --> 14:31.500
I think you've got an idea there.

14:31.500 --> 14:32.500
I hope so.

14:32.500 --> 14:38.500
When the dry deal come to eat, they want to see that bird sitting on the table, not at it.

14:38.500 --> 14:41.500
I'm so sorry to keep noble chiefs waiting.

14:41.500 --> 14:43.500
Has the knight in costume yet?

14:43.500 --> 14:46.500
If Indian thirsty, they'll say spa.

14:46.500 --> 14:49.500
Be happy, bring refreshment.

14:49.500 --> 14:51.500
Crazy, I'll have a beer.

14:51.500 --> 14:54.500
Make mine a dry martini.

14:54.500 --> 14:56.500
Indian water.

14:56.500 --> 14:58.500
Fire water?

14:58.500 --> 15:00.500
Very dry.

15:00.500 --> 15:03.500
Ten fire water to one vermouth.

15:03.500 --> 15:13.500
Hey, where are you going with Herman?

15:13.500 --> 15:16.500
Oh, just Romeo is taking him out in the woods to go hunting.

15:16.500 --> 15:18.500
Oh, good, he'll like that.

15:18.500 --> 15:19.500
You want to go hunting with us, Ellie?

15:19.500 --> 15:20.500
I brought granny's shotgun.

15:20.500 --> 15:22.500
Well, no, Ellie can't do that.

15:22.500 --> 15:24.500
She's got to take him to the wall I sent to her granny.

15:24.500 --> 15:26.500
And then she's got to go find her granny some hickory nuts.

15:26.500 --> 15:27.500
Where, pa?

15:27.500 --> 15:30.500
I ain't seen a single hickory tree on this whole place.

15:30.500 --> 15:33.500
Why don't you try over to the dry, dear, they might just have one.

15:33.500 --> 15:34.500
Yes, sir.

15:34.500 --> 15:36.500
Why, they ain't got none, do they?

15:36.500 --> 15:38.500
Hurry with the hickory nuts, sir.

15:38.500 --> 15:39.500
Drive on, Jethro.

15:39.500 --> 15:40.500
Bye, Ellie.

15:40.500 --> 15:41.500
Bye.

15:41.500 --> 15:43.500
Have a good time, Herman.

15:43.500 --> 15:44.500
There.

15:44.500 --> 15:50.500
We're going to have a very authentic reproduction of the first Thanksgiving.

15:50.500 --> 15:52.500
Nasa soites?

15:52.500 --> 15:54.500
And you are squanto.

15:54.500 --> 15:56.500
No, lady.

15:56.500 --> 16:01.500
That's the clown that rides around with the lone ranger.

16:01.500 --> 16:05.500
Oh, ho, ho, ho, hi-ho.

16:05.500 --> 16:07.500
No, I won't do it.

16:07.500 --> 16:09.500
Please, no, pa.

16:09.500 --> 16:12.500
Not half my picture taken in this ridiculous getup.

16:12.500 --> 16:14.500
But it'll be in the society section tomorrow.

16:14.500 --> 16:16.500
Oh, no, it won't.

16:16.500 --> 16:20.500
Please, redskin help, pale-faced squaw.

16:20.500 --> 16:24.500
Pale-faced squaw, speak with forked tongue.

16:24.500 --> 16:27.500
I will bring redskin firewater.

16:27.500 --> 16:31.500
But, Miss Hathaway, quickly, one beer and one firewater.

16:31.500 --> 16:34.500
I mean, dry martini.

16:34.500 --> 16:36.500
I could use one of those myself.

16:36.500 --> 16:37.500
Grab him.

16:37.500 --> 16:38.500
No, no, no.

16:38.500 --> 16:40.500
Oh, no, no, I'll fix you a drink myself.

16:40.500 --> 16:42.500
I just don't want you disappearing again.

16:42.500 --> 16:43.500
Let go of me.

16:43.500 --> 16:44.500
Sorry, mister.

16:44.500 --> 16:47.500
You no pose, we no get paid.

16:47.500 --> 16:54.500
Still, I'll get you.

16:54.500 --> 16:57.500
Ah, oh.

16:57.500 --> 16:58.500
It's gone.

16:58.500 --> 17:00.500
Can she roast a turkey brown?

17:00.500 --> 17:02.500
Billy boy, Billy boy.

17:02.500 --> 17:05.500
Can she roast a turkey brown?

17:05.500 --> 17:06.500
Charmin' Billy.

17:06.500 --> 17:09.500
She can roast a turkey brown.

17:09.500 --> 17:11.500
Quick as jizz, shoots him down.

17:11.500 --> 17:13.500
She's a young thing and...

17:13.500 --> 17:15.500
Granny, granny, get the gun.

17:15.500 --> 17:17.500
Engines, get your guns.

17:17.500 --> 17:18.500
The engines is attacking.

17:18.500 --> 17:19.500
Engines?

17:19.500 --> 17:21.500
You mean redskin savages?

17:21.500 --> 17:23.500
Yes, ma'am, they zone the warpath.

17:23.500 --> 17:24.500
They done captured Mr. Drysdale.

17:24.500 --> 17:25.500
Where's my gun, Annie?

17:25.500 --> 17:27.500
Annie, where is my gun, Annie?

17:27.500 --> 17:31.500
Oh, General took it to the woods with him this morning when him and Pa went hunting.

17:31.500 --> 17:33.500
Ah, just like my granny always said.

17:33.500 --> 17:38.500
Them redskins wait until the menfolk disappear with the guns and then they attack.

17:38.500 --> 17:39.500
What'll we do, granny?

17:39.500 --> 17:41.500
We'll fight them tooth and nail.

17:41.500 --> 17:43.500
We'll sell our scouts' deer.

17:43.500 --> 17:46.500
Come on, child, but protect your hair.

18:02.500 --> 18:07.500
Now then, happy pilgrims, happy Al Gantwin.

18:10.500 --> 18:12.500
Chief, you look terrible.

18:12.500 --> 18:14.500
What do you want for this kind of dope?

18:14.500 --> 18:16.500
Custer's last day in...

18:16.500 --> 18:19.500
No, no, no, I meant my chief, Mr. Drysdale.

18:20.500 --> 18:23.500
Now then, everyone smile.

18:23.500 --> 18:25.500
Smile.

18:25.500 --> 18:27.500
Hold it, hold it, hold it, hold it.

18:28.500 --> 18:29.500
Excellent.

18:29.500 --> 18:30.500
Goodbye, all.

18:30.500 --> 18:33.500
Wait, Milford, the next is the most important picture of all.

18:33.500 --> 18:35.500
We have the Indians give us the turkey.

18:35.500 --> 18:36.500
Oh, Margaret.

18:36.500 --> 18:38.500
I shall reload the camera while you get the bird.

18:39.500 --> 18:40.500
Where is the turkey?

18:40.500 --> 18:42.500
It was in a crate right over there.

18:42.500 --> 18:44.500
Well, that's the one you mean I gave to the clamboyants.

18:44.500 --> 18:46.500
You what?

18:46.500 --> 18:48.500
Well, they did invite us over for Thanksgiving dinner.

18:48.500 --> 18:49.500
It seemed the least I could do.

18:49.500 --> 18:52.500
I am not eating with those dreadful people.

18:52.500 --> 18:55.500
Then I am not posing for these dreadful pictures.

18:55.500 --> 18:59.500
Yes, Milford, go get the turkey back.

18:59.500 --> 19:01.500
Oh, they'll think I'm an Indian giver.

19:02.500 --> 19:04.500
No offense, fellas.

19:04.500 --> 19:06.500
Then I'll get it.

19:06.500 --> 19:08.500
Oh, no, I'm not going to have you insulting them.

19:08.500 --> 19:12.500
Look, tell us where the turkey is and we'll get it.

19:12.500 --> 19:13.500
At the clampest.

19:13.500 --> 19:15.500
Right through that hedge.

19:15.500 --> 19:16.500
Right through there?

19:16.500 --> 19:17.500
Come on, friend, right through there.

19:17.500 --> 19:19.500
Tell them you just want to borrow it.

19:19.500 --> 19:20.500
They can have it back.

19:20.500 --> 19:21.500
What?

19:32.500 --> 19:34.500
Two of them coming through the hedge.

19:34.500 --> 19:36.500
They hate totem guns.

19:36.500 --> 19:38.500
We'll hide and ambush them.

19:38.500 --> 19:41.500
Remember now, don't let them grab your hair.

19:47.500 --> 19:48.500
Stop him!

19:54.500 --> 19:56.500
Where'd my buddy go?

19:56.500 --> 19:58.500
Come on, that's mine.

19:58.500 --> 19:59.500
That's mine.

20:03.500 --> 20:05.500
Well, I reckon we got to go in and face Granny.

20:05.500 --> 20:07.500
She's going to be powerful mad.

20:07.500 --> 20:09.500
Yeah, but Ellie's going to be powerful happy.

20:09.500 --> 20:11.500
With Herman, that's two happies to one mad.

20:11.500 --> 20:13.500
I'm kind of happy myself.

20:13.500 --> 20:15.500
So am I, Jethro.

20:15.500 --> 20:16.500
Well, let's get him around the back.

20:16.500 --> 20:18.500
You fetch the gun.

20:20.500 --> 20:22.500
Come on, Herman.

20:22.500 --> 20:33.500
Outside of a politician, I ain't never seen any critter so all-fired anxious to shake a man's hand.

20:33.500 --> 20:35.500
Keep a sharp lookout, Ellie.

20:35.500 --> 20:37.500
Them redskins is tricky rascals.

20:37.500 --> 20:38.500
Yes, I'm Granny.

20:42.500 --> 20:43.500
Keep quiet, you!

20:43.500 --> 20:46.500
Don't you try to signal the rest of your tribe.

20:47.500 --> 20:48.500
Lay down!

20:48.500 --> 20:50.500
You hair-spilling varmint!

20:50.500 --> 20:52.500
Granny, I hear something.

20:54.500 --> 20:56.500
It's a turkey gobble.

20:57.500 --> 20:59.500
There it is again.

20:59.500 --> 21:01.500
Don't let it fool you, child.

21:01.500 --> 21:03.500
That's an old Indian trick.

21:03.500 --> 21:05.500
Sneaking up to attack?

21:05.500 --> 21:09.500
Elkie, tell it comes pawing Jethro with the gloves.

21:09.500 --> 21:10.500
We're shamed!

21:10.500 --> 21:11.500
We're shamed, Ellie!

21:11.500 --> 21:12.500
We're shamed!

21:12.500 --> 21:13.500
We're shamed!

21:13.500 --> 21:14.500
We're shamed!

21:14.500 --> 21:15.500
We're shamed!

21:15.500 --> 21:16.500
We're shamed!

21:16.500 --> 21:17.500
We're shamed!

21:17.500 --> 21:18.500
We're shamed!

21:18.500 --> 21:19.500
We're shamed!

21:19.500 --> 21:20.500
We're shamed!

21:21.500 --> 21:22.500
Oh, quick!

21:22.500 --> 21:24.500
Let him in before they get a hairy-in-the-back!

21:24.500 --> 21:27.500
Now you're going to get it, you red devils!

21:29.500 --> 21:31.500
Granny, what concernation you got here?

21:31.500 --> 21:33.500
Some blood-thirsty savages!

21:33.500 --> 21:34.500
That's what we got here!

21:34.500 --> 21:37.500
And don't you never go off and leave us without any firearms

21:37.500 --> 21:40.500
unless you want to come home and find a couple of bald-headed women!

21:40.500 --> 21:43.500
Yeah, they tried to scam us, but we jumped them first!

21:43.500 --> 21:47.500
Well, how in the world could there be wild Indians in Beverly Hills?

21:47.500 --> 21:52.000
Any place that can have them honoree looking billiards can have Injun.

21:54.000 --> 21:58.000
The next time you get me a job like this I want stuntman's pay.

22:00.500 --> 22:04.000
Injun, I reckon you better stay with my Uncle J. It says you can go.

22:08.000 --> 22:11.000
Oy vey is me!

22:11.000 --> 22:16.000
Anybody understand Injun talk?

22:19.000 --> 22:22.000
Here, have a slug of drainage rheumatism medicine.

22:22.000 --> 22:23.000
Thank you.

22:23.000 --> 22:25.000
Get the kinks out of your joints.

22:25.000 --> 22:29.000
So, here you are lollygagging while we are waiting.

22:29.000 --> 22:32.000
And you always after the fire water.

22:32.000 --> 22:34.000
It will never work for me again.

22:34.000 --> 22:38.000
If I do, I want to read the script first.

22:38.000 --> 22:41.000
Me, me, me.

22:41.000 --> 22:43.000
Shame on you.

22:43.000 --> 22:45.000
I warned you not to come over here and insult these people.

22:45.000 --> 22:49.000
I am not insulting anybody. I am being insulted.

22:49.000 --> 22:52.000
What is keeping everybody? The turkey is right outside.

22:52.000 --> 22:54.000
Don't you dare touch her.

22:54.000 --> 22:56.000
Order, order.

22:56.000 --> 22:59.000
Lay down everybody.

23:00.000 --> 23:07.000
It seems to me that Thanksgiving is a mighty poor time for folks to be yelling and arguing at one another.

23:07.000 --> 23:11.000
Besides, we have a whole heap of misunderstandings to sort out.

23:11.000 --> 23:15.000
And I think we all ought to sit down and start sorting them out.

23:19.000 --> 23:21.000
Granny, this is mighty fine Thanksgiving vittles.

23:21.000 --> 23:24.000
Best tasting fish I ever ate.

23:24.000 --> 23:26.000
What do you say this is called again, Chief?

23:26.000 --> 23:29.000
Gefilten fish. My mother fixed it.

23:29.000 --> 23:34.000
I am dingy. You Indians sure do know how to cook.

23:34.000 --> 23:36.000
That's a fact, Granny.

23:36.000 --> 23:38.000
That there chicken soup was the best ever.

23:38.000 --> 23:40.000
Everything is delicious.

23:40.000 --> 23:42.000
Marvelous dinner.

23:42.000 --> 23:44.000
Delightful.

23:44.000 --> 23:47.000
Herman and me sure like it. Don't we, Herman?

23:53.000 --> 23:56.000
By the way, Mr. Clampett, do you know you're eating on a billiard table?

23:56.000 --> 23:58.000
Yes, sir, I do.

23:58.000 --> 24:04.000
Next year, we're going to have us one of them rascals for Thanksgiving.

24:04.000 --> 24:28.000
I still say I ain't going to cook it.

